<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Victoria</h1><p class="st-meta">1851 CE – 1901 CE · capital Melbourne</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Australian colony created in 1851 just as gold was found, which made Melbourne one of the richest cities in the world.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-tasmania">Van Diemen's Land</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-batman">John Batman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-pascoe-fawkner">John Pascoe Fawkner</a>, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> in 1835 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulin">Kulin</a> land, and the area was governed as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-phillip-district">Port Phillip District</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-new-south-wales">Colony of New South Wales</a>. Pressure for separation succeeded in 1851, when Victoria became its own colony under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-la-trobe">Charles La Trobe</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Weeks after separation, gold was found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballarat">Ballarat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bendigo">Bendigo</a>, and the population rose from about 77,000 to over 500,000 within a decade. Miners angry at licence fees rebelled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eureka-rebellion">Eureka Stockade</a> in 1854. Self-government came in 1855, and the secret ballot introduced in 1856 spread worldwide as the 'Australian ballot'. Wool, gold and manufacturing behind <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protective-tariff">protective tariffs</a> created the wealth of 'Marvellous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>' in the 1880s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A land boom collapsed into depression in the early 1890s. Victorian politicians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-deakin">Alfred Deakin</a> led the movement for federation, and Victoria became a state of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia</a> in 1901. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> served as the temporary national capital until 1927.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-victoria.jpg" alt="Colony of Victoria" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Victoria_(1877%E2%80%931901).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1851 CE – 1901 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Melbourne</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles La Trobe, Charles Hotham</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism, Roman Catholicism, Presbyterianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Victoria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1876&amp;focus=colony-of-victoria" aria-label="Colony of Victoria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8297 3537 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of Victoria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8617 3793L8856 3661M8617 3793L4997 651M8617 3793L8774 3709"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8856 3661h0M4997 651h0M8774 3709h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8617 3793h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8617 3793h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Melbourne. <a href="/atlas?year=1876&amp;focus=colony-of-victoria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
